{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they journeyed from Rephidim.</b> It is difficult: why did it delay the earlier event, for this verse should have come before the preceding verse that says ״they came to the wilderness of Sinai״? Perhaps it is along the lines of what they said (Sanhedrin 105b): love upsets the order to give precedence to what is later — for since this is the day longed for by the Creator for the Torah, for the world, for the supernal beings and the lower beings, and from the day of creation they sit and wait: when will Israel arrive in the wilderness of Sinai? Therefore when they arrived there, they could not hold back [long enough] to narrate the order of events, and immediately the announcement took precedence in His saying <b>on this day they came</b> etc. — the Beloved and the desired One reached the yearning and the yearner, and the heavens and the earth rejoiced, for this is the purpose of creation and its hope. And afterward the verse returned to inform us of the details of the events." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to raise another difficulty regarding His saying <b>and they came to the wilderness of Sinai</b> — but the verse already said just before it ״they came to the wilderness of Sinai.״ Further, one must know why it was necessary to say ״and they encamped in the wilderness״ — is the matter not self-evident, for at the place of their arrival they encamped?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is to set forth three matters that are the core principles of preparation for receiving the Torah, through which HaShem was reconciled to bequeath them the heritage of Shaddai, which is our pleasant Torah." "<b>The first</b> is fortitude and strength in the labor of Torah, for laziness is the weed that destroys attainment. And so you will find that wherever HaShem mentions the Torah, He is precise to use language of strength and might — to the point that one would die over it, as it is written (Numbers 19:14) ״this is the Torah: a man when he dies״ etc., and our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded it (Shabbat 83b) and so forth. Likewise, our Sages, of blessed memory, are precise when mentioning it to say ״the labor of Torah,״ ״the exertion of Torah,״ and many like these. And see what I explained in my book ״Chefetz HaShem,״ which I composed in my youth on several tractances of the Talmud, regarding the saying of our Sages, of blessed memory (Shabbat 88b), ״to those who affirm it״ etc., and you will see that the attainment is not achieved except through great exertion. Corresponding to this, the verse says <b>and they journeyed from Rephidim</b> — it does not come to inform us of the place from which they journeyed, for if so it should have set it before their encampments; rather, the intent is to say that they departed from the attribute of weakening of the hands, as we find that our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded thus (Sanhedrin 106a) on the verse (Exodus 17:8) ״and he fought with Israel in Rephidim״ — בִּרְפִידִים, in the weakening of the hands, end of quote. And now they departed from this attribute and prepared themselves to perform the labor of bearing [the yoke] in the pleasantness of HaShem, and this is His saying <b>and they came to the wilderness of Sinai.</b>" "<b>And the second</b> matter is lowliness and humility, for words of Torah endure only in one who humbles himself and makes himself like a wilderness. Corresponding to this, He said <b>and they encamped in the wilderness</b> — meaning, an expression of lowliness and humility like the wilderness, which everyone tramples upon." "<b>And the third matter</b> is the quality of the appointment of the wise in fellowship with a whole and perfect heart — not that they should be solitary individuals, about whom Scripture said (Jeremiah 50:36) ״חֶרֶב אֶל הַבַּדִּים״ (a sword against the solitary ones) (B'rakhot 63a) — but rather they should convene together and join one to another and show a friendly face one to another. Corresponding to this the verse said <b>and Israel encamped there</b> in the singular — for they all became together as one person, and now they were worthy to receive the Torah."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:03.981731772Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}